course: dictionary
lesson: 692
title: Jumping on the bandwagon
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bandwagon has two meanings. It is a large cart that once carried a band playing music in a parade. It is also something that suddenly becomes popular, which many people join just because others do.
explain: When you «jump on the bandwagon», you do something only because everyone else is doing it. «Everyone does it» is a weak reason by itself, because lots of people can be wrong at the same time.
explain: Check a reason by asking why, not just who else is joining in. Thinking for yourself means choosing something because you have good reasons for it.
fact: A bandwagon is a large cart that carried a band playing music in a parade.
fact: The idiom «jump on the bandwagon» means to join something only because it has suddenly become popular.
fact: The «bandwagon effect» is a trick: «everyone is buying it, so you should too».
fact: The bandwagon trap, also called appeal to popularity, treats how many people do something as proof that it is right.
fact: Views and likes measure attention, not truth.
exercise: Which sentence shows someone jumping on the bandwagon? | I compared the prices and picked the best pair | I read the reviews before I chose these trainers | I am buying these trainers because my old ones are broken | *I am buying these trainers because everyone in my class has them
exercise: Ben says, «Lots of people liked the video, so it must be true.» What is Ben using? | *The bandwagon trap | A quiet whisper | A large cart for a parade | An ancient argument
exercise: «Everyone does it» is a weak reason by itself. Why? | *Lots of people can be wrong at the same time | It means the same as a parade | It is always rude to say it | It is too short to count as a reason
test: What does the bandwagon trap say? | A good reason needs no thinking at all | *Lots of people do it or say it, so it must be right | Only one person can know the truth | A band should always lead the parade
test: What is a good question to ask before you jump on a bandwagon? | Can I join the parade? | How many likes can I get? | Who is the most popular person here? | *Why is this true, and do I have good reasons?
exam: Ella says, «Ten thousand people shared it, so the claim must be true.» What trick is Ella using, and what should she ask instead? | *The bandwagon trap, so she should ask whether the claim is true | Quiet whispering, so she should speak louder | A petrol station, so she should check the map | A parade cart, so she should ask for a band
